WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Speaker 1: Welcome to the show. I'm producer&nbsp;
Pat Forde, filling in for David today as he is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.480
traveling up in Canada, visiting our neighbors to&nbsp;
the north. He has some family members he's seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:13.440
up in Montreal. So it's going to be me hosting&nbsp;
today as well as on Monday. And then we actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:18.480
a foreign cousins who's going to step in and guest&nbsp;
host a couple of shows for us next week. We do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.720
have a jam packed show to get to today. We're&nbsp;
going to be discussing everything from the Mar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:28.720
a Lago raid to the new IRS agents to Alex Jones&nbsp;
to Marjorie Taylor GREENE. But let's start things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:33.680
off today by talking about how it's looking even&nbsp;
more unlikely that Joe Biden will be running for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:39.920
a second term in 2024. Now, even back when Biden&nbsp;
was campaigning in 2020, I thought that Biden is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:45.360
just going to be a one term president if he wins.&nbsp;
He's convinced himself that he is the best shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:50.960
at defeating Donald Trump and getting our country&nbsp;
back on a path to normalcy. So he'll when his one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:55.680
term, he'll accomplish whatever he can and then&nbsp;
he'll step aside and let someone else take over.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:00.480
And the real reason is his age, right? Biden&nbsp;
would be 82 by the time that he's sworn in for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:06.640
a second term and 86 by the time he'd leave office&nbsp;
after eight years. I'm not trying to be a just or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:11.200
anything like that, but there's just a reality to&nbsp;
the fact that the president of United States is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:16.160
the most important job in the world. You could&nbsp;
argue it's it's one of most stressful jobs out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:21.040
there. And you're always busy. You have to make&nbsp;
these public appearances. Not everyone even lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:25.840
in their eighties, let alone is working into their&nbsp;
eighties, let alone has the most important job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:30.800
arguably in the entire world in their eighties. So&nbsp;
I don't know if Joe Biden really wants to do that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:35.200
And over the past couple of years, whenever a&nbsp;
reporter has asked about Biden running again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:40.320
for a second term, the White House position&nbsp;
has always been that Biden intends to run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:45.200
but they never make the full on commitment,&nbsp;
leaving them plenty of room to backtrack. If they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:49.040
decide that, you know, he's not really going&nbsp;
to be doing this. The one time that Kamala&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:53.520
Harris overstepped and said that he is going&nbsp;
to be running her office, had to step back the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:58.240
statement the next day. Well, now we're hearing&nbsp;
from Democratic Representative Carolyn Maloney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:03.680
who represents part of New York City, that off&nbsp;
the record, Joe Biden is not running for president&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:09.200
again. Maloney was speaking to The New York Times&nbsp;
editorial board and the exchange went like this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:14.560
New York Times's Eleanor Randolph said, quote,&nbsp;
Should President Biden run again? Maloney said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:19.680
off the record, he's not running again. New&nbsp;
York Times's Jodi thought men definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:25.280
mispronouncing. That said. Not off the record.&nbsp;
On the record, Maloney said. On the record, no,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:30.480
he should not run again. So there was a bit&nbsp;
of confusion about this on the record versus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:36.240
off the record thing. Both the reporter and the&nbsp;
interviewee are supposed to confirm and agree that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.640
something is off the record before it's truly off&nbsp;
the record, which you can see didn't happen here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:45.760
in this exchange. But her position sure sounded&nbsp;
like Biden won't be running again for a second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:51.760
term. She also doesn't want him to run again. As&nbsp;
to why Maloney might not want Biden to run again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:56.640
that's probably because of his sagging approval&nbsp;
numbers. They haven't been doing all that well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:00.160
in recent months, although it has ticked up&nbsp;
a little bit over the past couple of weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:06.560
he's back to around 40%. But maybe Maloney just&nbsp;
sees Biden as a liability for the Democrats and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:12.880
she wants him to step aside and to get a new face&nbsp;
in there. Maloney later went on CNN to apologize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:17.213
for the statement and to say that Biden is a&nbsp;
great president and she doesn't think that Biden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.213 --> 00:03:22.000
is going to run again, but she sure wants him to.
Speaker 2: Mr. President, I apologize. I want you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:27.440
to run. I happen to think you won't be running,&nbsp;
but when you run or if you run, I will be there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:33.840
100%. You have deserved it. You are a great&nbsp;
president and thank you for everything you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:38.800
done for my state and all the states and all&nbsp;
the cities in America. Thank you, Mr. President.&nbsp;

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:43.600
Speaker 1: Okay. So a little awkward there, and&nbsp;
it's a little bit unbelievable, too, because while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:48.480
she is holding firm that she doesn't believe Biden&nbsp;
will be running again, she also doesn't sound that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:52.640
sincere that she wants Biden to run again. I&nbsp;
happen to believe her earlier comments about it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:57.760
are true rather than this apology and correction.&nbsp;
And the real question in this conversation is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:02.240
do Democrats have a better chance at holding the&nbsp;
White House if it's Joe Biden at the top of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:07.440
ticket in 2024 versus if it's someone else, fill&nbsp;
in the blank. You know, it could be Kamala Harris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:12.160
Gavin Newsom, Elizabeth Warren, Pete Buttigieg,&nbsp;
Edge, whoever. I could be swayed either way on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:18.032
this, because helping Biden, of course, is the&nbsp;
power of incumbency. Presidents do tend to win&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.032 --> 00:04:22.400
reelection more often than not. But then there's&nbsp;
also these liabilities, right? Like there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:27.120
Biden's advanced age. That will be even more of&nbsp;
a liability the next time around because he'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:31.920
be four years older. There's also his approval&nbsp;
ratings, which at least now aren't so good. Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:37.120
they'll improve between now and the next election.&nbsp;
There's also the question of if Republicans are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:42.560
able to take back the House and or Senate, will&nbsp;
Biden be able to get all that much accomplished?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:46.480
Probably not. So then that's hard to make the&nbsp;
case to the voters that you should reelect him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:52.080
So to me, it seems a little bit too early to tell&nbsp;
what's going to happen for sure. But I'm sticking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.160
with my position that I don't think that Joe&nbsp;
Biden is going to be running for president again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:01.200
in 2024. And you better believe. That that&nbsp;
will set us up for an interesting election&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:05.680
cycle if he chooses not to run again, because&nbsp;
then both the Democrats and the Republicans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:11.360
will have to be having these contested primaries&nbsp;
to see who their respective nominees will be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:14.400
And that will definitely be&nbsp;
entertaining and juicy to cover.

